  "geometryBasis": "The named National Trust archaeological site, its exposed stone wall traces, separate standing building polygons and the surveyed octagonal spring cistern establish the extent and the orientation.",
  "sectionBasis": "Exact Qualla bathhouse and corridor photographs show low coursed stone. The supplemental creator releases show the surfaced ruins and capped walls, lodge and cistern. National Trust and the architect document the standing range and building materials.",
  "limitations": [
    "This is the surviving site, with exposed foundations, standing shelters, and the Victorian lodge and museum. The Roman superstructure and interior mosaics have no rendered reconstruction.",
    "The open Roman wall lines, octagonal spring basin and standing buildings use native map geometry. Wall heights, roof sections, floor levels, stone capping and fenestration are estimates. The modern shelter is generalized from its mapped footprint and written project facts.",
    "The regional DEM anchors the site and the natural slope. Models of the wall and basin courses sit above this independent field. The adjoining reception, learning pavilion, gardens and buried archaeology remain outside the model."
